      "math_academy_id": "q-129151",
      "sequence_position": 1,
      "problem": "Calculate ${\\int }_{1}^{2}[f(t)+\\frac{5}{3}g(t)]\\text{d}t$ given that ${\\int }_{1}^{2}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=⟨-2,\\,1,\\,4⟩$ and ${\\int }_{1}^{2}g(t)\\,\\text{d}t=⟨-3,\\,6,\\,0⟩.$",
      "worked_solution": "From the vector addition and scalar multiplication rules, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{2}[f(t)+\\frac{5}{3}g(t)]\\text{d}t={\\int }_{1}^{2}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t+\\frac{5}{3}{\\int }_{1}^{2}g(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}[f(t)+\\frac{5}{3}g(t)]\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t+\\frac{5}{3}{\\int }_{1}^{2}g(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨-2,\\,1,\\,4⟩+\\frac{5}{3}⟨-3,\\,6,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2,\\,1,\\,4⟩+⟨-5,\\,10,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-7,\\,11,\\,4⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
